This project analyzes the correlation between step count and sleep quality.
Given this topic, I decided to collect my own data, using my personal step count for the day and the amount of sleep I would get that night.
I collected my step count over a span of 26 days as a barrier I encountered was that I could only access my sleep data for those days.
I input them into an Excel sheet and I then converted the sleep length from hours and minutes to just minutes. 

With the optimal formatting, I input the .csv file into PyCharm for further analysis with Python.
There, I ran a script to gain the statistical descriptions as well as the correlations.
I found the mean, max, min, quartiles, and count as well as the t and p values.
My average step count was about 6938 steps a day and my average sleep length was about 6 hours and 42 minutes a night.
The minimum number of steps I got was 355 and the man was 22,253 steps.
The minimum amount of sleep I got was 4 hours and 55 minutes while the maximum was 8 hours and 30 minutes.

Using the Pearson testing the linear relationship resulting in the values of  t=-0.302 and p-value=0.3575 and the T-test resulting in the values of p=0.7652 and r=-0.188 showed that there was no correlation between the step count and sleep length as they both exceeded the p-value standard of p < 0.05.
Initially, I hypothesized that a higher step count would increase the amount of steps taken that day and my hypothesis was rejected by the p-values.

To create a visualization of these results, I imported my .csv file to Tableau where I used a bar chart to show the comparison of the two data categories.
Seeing the two categories side-by-side further illustrates the lack of correlation between them. 

